N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition; this is the compositional audit. Every bar totals four eighths (2/4). Bar 1 A4 A4 eighths, F#4 quarter; bar 2 rest; bar 3 F#4 E4 eighths, D4 quarter; bar 4 rest; bar 5 A4 F#4 A4 F#4 eighths; bar 6 E4 E4 eighths, D4 quarter; bar 7 rest; bar 8 D4 quarter, quarter rest. Pitch set D-E-F#-A, range D4-A4; sung intervals are steps and minor thirds, and the only larger move is the re-entry on A4 after the silent bar 4 (the pitch the song began on). The earlier draft used the same E4-G4 two-note shape, six sung notes and one unsung note as "Bamboo Breath"; this version is rebuilt around short falling eighth-note drops and silent gaps, while "Bamboo Breath" is now held tones in a continuous phrase. The analyzer reads level 2 (rests, pentatonic); its Ontario line reads Grade 5 only because it counts the two-sharp key signature, which JK children never read. Originality: so-mi and mi-re-do fragments are common property; checked against "Rain, Rain", "Bounce High, Bounce Low", "Hot Cross Buns", "Twinkle, Twinkle" and "Frère Jacques", with no shared opening. Glennie's "A Little Prayer" is a slow chordal marimba piece; nothing of its melody or harmony is used.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether four-year-olds can strike softly and wait, whether a two-beat gap at quarter = 72 is long enough for a bell to fade (the teacher may slow down or repeat a gap), whether the A4 re-entry after silence is pitched securely, and whether the framing of Glennie is right. A classroom trial, an accessibility-minded educator and a second score reader must settle these.
